1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,541
At 12:51 in the afternoon, on an ordinary
Tuesday, the ground beneath a city of

2
00:00:06,541 --> 00:00:12,480
nearly four hundred thousand people moved
upward faster than gravity could pull

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,398
it down.

4
00:00:13,898 --> 00:00:16,050
People were lifted off their feet.

5
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:18,013
Cars bounced on the road.

6
00:00:18,092 --> 00:00:22,998
In some places, the ground jumped with
more than twice the force that keeps us

7
00:00:22,998 --> 00:00:24,428
standing on Earth.

8
00:00:24,928 --> 00:00:26,814
It lasted only seconds.

9
00:00:27,114 --> 00:00:31,865
But when it stopped, a city that had
already survived one earthquake would never

10
00:00:31,865 --> 00:00:33,347
look the same again.

11
00:00:33,847 --> 00:00:36,762
This is the story of
Christchurch, New Zealand.

12
00:00:36,841 --> 00:00:39,746
The day the ground came
back for a second time.

13
00:00:39,826 --> 00:00:44,746
And the strangest part is this: the
earthquake that did the most damage was not

14
00:00:44,746 --> 00:00:46,042
the biggest one.

15
00:00:46,121 --> 00:00:47,792
It wasn't even close.

16
00:00:48,292 --> 00:00:53,274
To understand why, we have to go back
five months, to a night when the whole

17
00:00:53,274 --> 00:00:54,625
city was asleep.

18
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:57,661
It's the fourth of September, 2010.

19
00:00:57,740 --> 00:00:59,871
4:35 in the morning.

20
00:01:00,371 --> 00:01:02,505
Most of Christchurch is in bed.

21
00:01:02,585 --> 00:01:04,284
The streets are empty.

22
00:01:04,584 --> 00:01:06,260
The bars have closed.

23
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,513
The buses aren't running yet.

24
00:01:09,013 --> 00:01:14,688
Then, about forty kilometers west of the
city, near a small farming town called

25
00:01:14,688 --> 00:01:17,209
Darfield, the Earth tears open.

26
00:01:17,709 --> 00:01:21,563
A fault nobody knew existed
rips through the farmland.

27
00:01:21,644 --> 00:01:27,034
Fences that once ran in straight lines
are suddenly bent sideways, shifted by

28
00:01:27,034 --> 00:01:29,703
several meters in a single moment.

29
00:01:29,784 --> 00:01:31,069
Roads crack.

30
00:01:31,368 --> 00:01:33,647
Railway lines twist like wire.

31
00:01:34,147 --> 00:01:36,757
The earthquake measures 7.1.

32
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:41,356
That's powerful enough to flatten
cities in many parts of the world.

33
00:01:41,856 --> 00:01:44,757
In Christchurch, chimneys
crash through roofs.

34
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:47,870
Old brick shopfronts
crumble onto the footpaths.

35
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:52,876
Water and sand burst up from the ground
in the eastern suburbs, flooding streets

36
00:01:52,876 --> 00:01:54,037
with gray mud.

37
00:01:54,537 --> 00:01:58,888
And yet, when the sun comes up,
something remarkable becomes clear.

38
00:01:59,388 --> 00:02:02,387
No one has been killed
directly by the shaking.

39
00:02:02,887 --> 00:02:05,003
New Zealand calls it a miracle.

40
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:06,987
And in many ways, it was.

41
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:08,914
The timing saved lives.

42
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:15,013
At 4:35 in the morning, nobody was
walking under those falling facades.

43
00:02:15,092 --> 00:02:18,673
Nobody was sitting in the
cafés that lost their walls.

44
00:02:19,173 --> 00:02:21,683
But that miracle carried a hidden cost.

45
00:02:22,183 --> 00:02:26,842
Because when a city survives something
that terrible without a single direct

46
00:02:26,842 --> 00:02:30,360
death, people start to
believe the danger has passed.

47
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:34,109
Over the following months,
the ground kept trembling.

48
00:02:34,188 --> 00:02:37,132
Thousands of aftershocks
rattled Christchurch.

49
00:02:37,211 --> 00:02:39,406
Some were small enough to sleep through.

50
00:02:39,485 --> 00:02:43,457
Others sent people running into
the street in the middle of dinner.

51
00:02:43,957 --> 00:02:46,283
Engineers inspected buildings.

52
00:02:46,584 --> 00:02:48,591
Many were given the all-clear.

53
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:50,002
Cracks were patched.

54
00:02:50,302 --> 00:02:51,670
Shops reopened.

55
00:02:51,971 --> 00:02:54,186
Life slowly returned to normal.

56
00:02:54,686 --> 00:02:57,508
The city told itself the worst was over.

57
00:02:58,008 --> 00:02:59,078
It wasn't.

58
00:02:59,828 --> 00:03:04,736
To understand what was coming, you need
to understand the ground Christchurch is

59
00:03:04,736 --> 00:03:05,592
built on.

60
00:03:06,092 --> 00:03:09,900
The city sits on flat land
between the mountains and the sea.

61
00:03:09,979 --> 00:03:15,312
Long before it was a city, this area
was swamp and wetland, crossed by rivers

62
00:03:15,312 --> 00:03:20,782
that carried sand, silt and gravel down
from the Southern Alps for thousands of

63
00:03:20,782 --> 00:03:21,512
years.

64
00:03:22,012 --> 00:03:24,205
That soft, wet ground is loose.

65
00:03:24,286 --> 00:03:27,167
Water fills the spaces between the grains.

66
00:03:27,667 --> 00:03:31,307
On a normal day, it holds
buildings up just fine.

67
00:03:31,388 --> 00:03:35,432
But when it's shaken hard enough,
something terrifying happens.

68
00:03:35,513 --> 00:03:40,633
The water pressure rises, the grains
lose their grip on each other, and solid

69
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:43,377
ground starts behaving like a liquid.

70
00:03:43,877 --> 00:03:46,013
Scientists call it liquefaction.

71
00:03:46,763 --> 00:03:48,073
Houses tilt.

72
00:03:48,372 --> 00:03:49,528
Cars sink.

73
00:03:49,829 --> 00:03:55,046
Roads split open, and gray slurry bubbles
up from below like the Earth itself is

74
00:03:55,046 --> 00:03:55,877
boiling.

75
00:03:56,377 --> 00:03:59,242
Christchurch had already
seen this in September.

76
00:03:59,323 --> 00:04:03,407
But it was about to see it on
a scale nobody had imagined.

77
00:04:03,907 --> 00:04:07,046
And to the south-east of
the city sat something else.

78
00:04:07,546 --> 00:04:08,893
The Port Hills.

79
00:04:09,193 --> 00:04:14,622
A ridge of rock that rises above the
suburbs, the remains of an ancient volcano

80
00:04:14,622 --> 00:04:17,519
that went quiet millions of years ago.

81
00:04:17,598 --> 00:04:21,276
On the other side lies the
harbor town of Lyttelton.

82
00:04:21,776 --> 00:04:22,838
Beautiful.

83
00:04:23,139 --> 00:04:24,055
Peaceful.

84
00:04:24,355 --> 00:04:25,338
Solid.

85
00:04:25,838 --> 00:04:27,793
Or so everyone believed.

86
00:04:28,543 --> 00:04:33,276
Because buried beneath those hills,
hidden under layers of rock, was another

87
00:04:33,276 --> 00:04:33,958
fault.

88
00:04:34,038 --> 00:04:36,249
One that didn't appear on any map.

89
00:04:36,329 --> 00:04:40,949
One that had likely been stressed and
loaded by the September earthquake.

90
00:04:41,449 --> 00:04:43,357
And it was running out of time.

91
00:04:43,857 --> 00:04:47,435
Tuesday, the twenty-second
of February, 2011.

92
00:04:47,935 --> 00:04:50,024
It's late summer in New Zealand.

93
00:04:50,105 --> 00:04:51,692
The weather is mild.

94
00:04:51,992 --> 00:04:54,110
The city center is busy again.

95
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:57,403
Office workers are stepping out for lunch.

96
00:04:57,483 --> 00:04:59,798
Shoppers move along Colombo Street.

97
00:04:59,877 --> 00:05:02,334
Buses stop and start through the traffic.

98
00:05:02,415 --> 00:05:07,819
Tourists climb the tower of ChristChurch
Cathedral, the stone church that stands

99
00:05:07,819 --> 00:05:13,208
at the very heart of the city, the
building the city itself is named around.

100
00:05:13,708 --> 00:05:19,576
A few blocks away, on Madras Street,
stands a six-story office block known as

101
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:21,182
the CTV building.

102
00:05:21,262 --> 00:05:26,182
It's home to a television station, a
medical clinic, and on one of its upper

103
00:05:26,182 --> 00:05:28,158
floors, a language school.

104
00:05:28,658 --> 00:05:32,829
Inside that school are dozens
of international students.

105
00:05:32,908 --> 00:05:37,773
Young people from Japan, China, the
Philippines and other countries.

106
00:05:37,853 --> 00:05:42,755
Many of them have come to New Zealand
to learn English, to build a future, to

107
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:44,829
see a new part of the world.

108
00:05:45,329 --> 00:05:46,701
Some are in class.

109
00:05:47,002 --> 00:05:48,879
Some are on their lunch break.

110
00:05:49,379 --> 00:05:55,064
Not far away stands the Pyne Gould
Corporation building, the PGC building.

111
00:05:55,144 --> 00:06:00,189
Five floors of offices, full of people
working through an ordinary day.

112
00:06:00,689 --> 00:06:05,163
Nobody in Christchurch knows that
the clock has almost run out.

113
00:06:05,663 --> 00:06:07,519
12:51 PM.

114
00:06:08,269 --> 00:06:13,423
Directly beneath the Port Hills, about
ten kilometers from the city center, the

115
00:06:13,423 --> 00:06:15,220
hidden fault gives way.

116
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:18,925
The earthquake is only magnitude 6.2.

117
00:06:19,005 --> 00:06:23,336
Far smaller than the 7.1
that struck in September.

118
00:06:23,836 --> 00:06:26,336
On paper, it should be the lesser threat.

119
00:06:26,836 --> 00:06:29,882
But this time, almost
everything is different.

120
00:06:30,382 --> 00:06:34,807
The rupture is shallow, just a
few kilometers below the surface.

121
00:06:34,887 --> 00:06:39,619
It's close, right on the city's
doorstep instead of out among the farms.

122
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:44,442
And it strikes in the middle of a working
day, when the city center is crowded.

123
00:06:44,942 --> 00:06:47,557
And then there's the
direction of the shaking.

124
00:06:48,057 --> 00:06:52,088
Most buildings are designed to
survive being pushed sideways.

125
00:06:52,168 --> 00:06:57,387
That's how engineers usually think about
earthquakes: the ground slides left and

126
00:06:57,387 --> 00:07:01,117
right, and the building has
to sway without breaking.

127
00:07:01,617 --> 00:07:06,108
But in Christchurch that afternoon,
the ground also punched upward.

128
00:07:06,608 --> 00:07:11,903
Instruments near the epicenter recorded
vertical shaking stronger than gravity

129
00:07:11,903 --> 00:07:12,690
itself.

130
00:07:12,771 --> 00:07:17,630
For a moment, the ground was rising
faster than objects could fall back down

131
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:18,449
onto it.

132
00:07:18,528 --> 00:07:23,375
Some of the highest ground accelerations
ever measured in a city were recorded

133
00:07:23,375 --> 00:07:24,237
that day.

134
00:07:24,737 --> 00:07:29,100
Imagine sitting at your desk and
feeling the floor slam up into you.

135
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:30,387
Not a sway.

136
00:07:30,687 --> 00:07:31,906
Not a rumble.

137
00:07:32,206 --> 00:07:34,069
A hammer blow from below.

138
00:07:34,569 --> 00:07:36,444
That is what Christchurch felt.

139
00:07:36,944 --> 00:07:40,074
In the city center, the
noise is overwhelming.

140
00:07:40,154 --> 00:07:45,146
A deep roar, then the crack of breaking
concrete, the shattering of glass, and

141
00:07:45,146 --> 00:07:47,291
the thunder of falling stone.

142
00:07:47,791 --> 00:07:53,098
The old brick and stone buildings, many
already weakened by September, begin to

143
00:07:53,098 --> 00:07:54,144
come apart.

144
00:07:54,644 --> 00:08:00,175
On Colombo Street, heavy facades peel
away from shopfronts and crash down onto

145
00:08:00,175 --> 00:08:01,406
the footpath.

146
00:08:01,485 --> 00:08:05,226
Two city buses are caught
beneath the falling masonry.

147
00:08:05,307 --> 00:08:10,036
People who had been simply riding through
town on their lunch hour are suddenly

148
00:08:10,036 --> 00:08:12,000
trapped under tons of brick.

149
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:17,375
At the center of the city, the spire of
ChristChurch Cathedral breaks off and

150
00:08:17,375 --> 00:08:19,689
collapses into the square below.

151
00:08:19,769 --> 00:08:24,574
The building that had stood as the
symbol of Christchurch for more than a

152
00:08:24,574 --> 00:08:26,968
century is torn open in seconds.

153
00:08:27,468 --> 00:08:29,964
Dust rises over the city center.

154
00:08:30,045 --> 00:08:31,641
Car alarms scream.

155
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:37,196
People pour out of buildings, many covered
in gray powder, some bleeding, some

156
00:08:37,196 --> 00:08:39,908
searching for anyone they came with.

157
00:08:40,408 --> 00:08:42,970
And then, two buildings fail completely.

158
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:46,269
The PGC building collapses.

159
00:08:46,570 --> 00:08:51,278
Its floors drop onto each other, folding
into a fraction of their original

160
00:08:51,278 --> 00:08:52,030
height.

161
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:57,604
People inside are trapped in pockets
between the slabs, in darkness, unable to

162
00:08:57,604 --> 00:08:58,270
move.

163
00:08:58,770 --> 00:09:03,207
And on Madras Street, the CTV
building doesn't just crack.

164
00:09:03,707 --> 00:09:04,812
It falls.

165
00:09:05,562 --> 00:09:08,734
In a matter of seconds,
six floors come down.

166
00:09:08,813 --> 00:09:14,270
Almost the entire structure collapses
into a pile of concrete and twisted steel.

167
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:19,706
Only one part of the building, a stairwell
tower on the side, is left standing.

168
00:09:20,206 --> 00:09:25,350
Inside that rubble are office workers,
patients, staff, and the students from

169
00:09:25,350 --> 00:09:26,984
the language school.

170
00:09:27,484 --> 00:09:31,879
Survivors in the street hear voices
calling from inside the wreckage.

171
00:09:32,379 --> 00:09:37,250
People begin climbing onto the rubble
with their bare hands, pulling at pieces

172
00:09:37,250 --> 00:09:38,302
of concrete.

173
00:09:38,383 --> 00:09:43,832
Off-duty workers, passers-by, police
officers and firefighters rush toward the

174
00:09:43,832 --> 00:09:44,496
site.

175
00:09:44,996 --> 00:09:49,355
But within minutes, they notice
something else rising from the ruins.

176
00:09:49,855 --> 00:09:50,826
Smoke.

177
00:09:51,576 --> 00:09:56,299
The fire started deep inside the
wreckage of the CTV building.

178
00:09:56,799 --> 00:10:01,559
At first it was a thin gray line of
smoke drifting up from the concrete.

179
00:10:01,639 --> 00:10:02,869
Then it grew.

180
00:10:03,369 --> 00:10:08,398
Rescuers were already on the pile,
listening for voices and digging toward

181
00:10:08,398 --> 00:10:10,325
anyone they could reach.

182
00:10:10,404 --> 00:10:14,696
A small number of people were
pulled out alive in the first hours.

183
00:10:14,775 --> 00:10:19,788
Some had been trapped between broken
floors, in tiny spaces where the concrete

184
00:10:19,788 --> 00:10:22,005
hadn't fully crushed together.

185
00:10:22,505 --> 00:10:27,333
But as the fire spread through the rubble,
the rescue became a desperate race.

186
00:10:27,413 --> 00:10:31,740
Teams fought the flames while trying
to reach people buried beneath them.

187
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:36,589
Heat, smoke and unstable concrete
made every movement dangerous.

188
00:10:37,089 --> 00:10:43,113
The CTV building became the single
deadliest place in the entire disaster.

189
00:10:43,613 --> 00:10:47,312
One hundred and fifteen
people lost their lives there.

190
00:10:47,393 --> 00:10:51,994
Many of them were the international
students from the language school, young

191
00:10:51,994 --> 00:10:56,783
people who had traveled across the
world to study, and who never came home.

192
00:10:57,283 --> 00:11:01,173
Their deaths turned a New Zealand
tragedy into a global one.

193
00:11:01,253 --> 00:11:06,543
Families in Japan, China, the Philippines
and many other countries waited for

194
00:11:06,543 --> 00:11:10,184
news that, for most of
them, would never be good.

195
00:11:10,684 --> 00:11:16,730
But the CTV building was only one of the
disasters unfolding across Christchurch

196
00:11:16,730 --> 00:11:18,176
that afternoon.

197
00:11:18,676 --> 00:11:24,452
Back at the PGC building, rescuers were
facing a different kind of challenge.

198
00:11:24,952 --> 00:11:27,961
The building had collapsed,
but not into a fire.

199
00:11:28,041 --> 00:11:31,698
Inside its folded floors,
people were still alive.

200
00:11:31,778 --> 00:11:35,414
Trapped in darkness, some
could send text messages.

201
00:11:35,495 --> 00:11:37,453
Some could tap on concrete.

202
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:39,004
Some could call out.

203
00:11:39,504 --> 00:11:44,144
Rescue teams cut through the slabs piece
by piece, working through the night.

204
00:11:44,224 --> 00:11:49,110
Over the following hours, survivors were
brought out of the wreckage one by one,

205
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:52,315
some of them after being
trapped for a full day.

206
00:11:52,815 --> 00:11:56,182
Eighteen people died in the PGC building.

207
00:11:56,261 --> 00:12:01,697
But many more walked out of it alive,
saved by the rescuers who refused to stop.

208
00:12:02,197 --> 00:12:06,955
And while all of this was happening in
the city center, the rest of Christchurch

209
00:12:06,955 --> 00:12:09,201
was falling apart in its own way.

210
00:12:09,701 --> 00:12:14,912
In the eastern suburbs, along the Avon
River, the ground turned to liquid.

211
00:12:15,412 --> 00:12:20,330
Within seconds of the shaking, gray silt
and water burst up through the soil.

212
00:12:20,409 --> 00:12:22,616
It poured out of cracks in the road.

213
00:12:22,697 --> 00:12:25,652
It flooded backyards
and filled living rooms.

214
00:12:25,731 --> 00:12:27,793
Cars sank up to their doors.

215
00:12:27,873 --> 00:12:29,304
Houses tilted.

216
00:12:29,605 --> 00:12:31,818
Footpaths buckled into waves.

217
00:12:32,318 --> 00:12:37,203
Streets that had been dry minutes
earlier were suddenly ankle-deep, then

218
00:12:37,203 --> 00:12:39,528
knee-deep, in thick, cold mud.

219
00:12:40,028 --> 00:12:41,523
Sewer pipes broke.

220
00:12:41,822 --> 00:12:43,392
Water mains burst.

221
00:12:43,692 --> 00:12:46,732
Power went out across
huge areas of the city.

222
00:12:47,232 --> 00:12:51,528
For thousands of families, home
was no longer safe to live in.

223
00:12:51,609 --> 00:12:53,442
And it wasn't just damaged.

224
00:12:53,522 --> 00:12:57,336
In some places, the land
underneath it had been ruined.

225
00:12:57,836 --> 00:13:03,197
Meanwhile, the Port Hills, the quiet
volcanic ridge that had hidden the fault

226
00:13:03,197 --> 00:13:05,854
all along, began to shed its rock.

227
00:13:06,354 --> 00:13:10,530
In seaside suburbs like Sumner
and Redcliffs, cliffs collapsed.

228
00:13:10,610 --> 00:13:16,120
Huge boulders, some the size of cars,
broke free and rolled down the hillsides.

229
00:13:16,201 --> 00:13:21,360
A few smashed straight through houses,
leaving holes in walls and roofs as if

230
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:24,058
the homes had been hit by artillery.

231
00:13:24,558 --> 00:13:29,715
The harbor town of Lyttelton, closest
to the epicenter, was badly hit too.

232
00:13:29,794 --> 00:13:32,599
Historic stone buildings
cracked and crumbled.

233
00:13:32,678 --> 00:13:38,206
The town's famous Timeball Station, a
landmark that had stood watch over the

234
00:13:38,206 --> 00:13:43,947
harbor since the 1870s, was so badly
damaged that it eventually had to be taken

235
00:13:43,947 --> 00:13:44,629
down.

236
00:13:45,129 --> 00:13:48,152
Back in the city, another
danger was growing.

237
00:13:48,652 --> 00:13:53,897
The Hotel Grand Chancellor, one of the
tallest buildings in Christchurch, had

238
00:13:53,897 --> 00:13:55,491
been badly damaged.

239
00:13:55,572 --> 00:13:59,777
Part of its structure had failed,
and the whole tower was now leaning.

240
00:13:59,856 --> 00:14:03,875
Emergency crews feared it could
collapse onto the streets below.

241
00:14:04,375 --> 00:14:07,279
Large areas around it were evacuated.

242
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:12,115
Rescuers had to work in its shadow,
never knowing whether the next aftershock

243
00:14:12,115 --> 00:14:13,645
would bring it down.

244
00:14:14,145 --> 00:14:16,327
And the aftershocks did keep coming.

245
00:14:16,408 --> 00:14:20,933
Each one sent fresh waves of panic
through a city already in shock.

246
00:14:21,433 --> 00:14:25,455
By the next day, the scale of
the disaster was undeniable.

247
00:14:25,955 --> 00:14:30,835
On the twenty-third of February, the New
Zealand government declared a state of

248
00:14:30,835 --> 00:14:32,304
national emergency.

249
00:14:32,384 --> 00:14:37,203
It was the first time in the country's
history that such a declaration had ever

250
00:14:37,203 --> 00:14:38,115
been made.

251
00:14:38,615 --> 00:14:43,317
Prime Minister John Key said this
could be New Zealand's darkest day.

252
00:14:43,817 --> 00:14:46,089
Help arrived from across the world.

253
00:14:46,168 --> 00:14:51,878
Search and rescue teams flew in from
Australia, Japan, the United States, the

254
00:14:51,878 --> 00:14:56,008
United Kingdom, Taiwan,
Singapore, China and beyond.

255
00:14:56,087 --> 00:15:01,331
Specialists with dogs, cameras and
listening equipment moved through the ruins,

256
00:15:01,331 --> 00:15:05,317
searching every collapsed
building for any sign of life.

257
00:15:05,817 --> 00:15:09,438
One question hung over the
cathedral in particular.

258
00:15:09,519 --> 00:15:14,489
For days, people feared that visitors
had been inside the tower when the spire

259
00:15:14,489 --> 00:15:15,431
came down.

260
00:15:15,510 --> 00:15:20,289
When searchers finally went through
the rubble, no one was found inside.

261
00:15:20,370 --> 00:15:24,977
It was one small piece of relief
in a week that offered very little.

262
00:15:25,477 --> 00:15:29,854
And then something remarkable started
happening in the eastern suburbs.

263
00:15:30,354 --> 00:15:33,188
Thousands of young people
showed up with shovels.

264
00:15:33,688 --> 00:15:38,616
University students, many of them
barely out of high school, organized

265
00:15:38,616 --> 00:15:43,378
themselves into what became known
as the Student Volunteer Army.

266
00:15:43,457 --> 00:15:48,469
They went street by street, house by
house, digging liquefaction silt out of

267
00:15:48,469 --> 00:15:52,753
yards and driveways for people
who couldn't do it themselves.

268
00:15:53,253 --> 00:15:56,714
Farmers arrived from the
countryside with machinery.

269
00:15:56,793 --> 00:16:01,128
Neighbors who had never spoken
shared food, water and toilets.

270
00:16:01,207 --> 00:16:03,402
Strangers checked on the elderly.

271
00:16:03,902 --> 00:16:05,498
The city was broken.

272
00:16:05,798 --> 00:16:07,351
But its people were not.

273
00:16:07,851 --> 00:16:15,190
In the end, the earthquake of the
twenty-second of February, 2011 took the lives

274
00:16:15,190 --> 00:16:16,798
of 185 people.

275
00:16:17,298 --> 00:16:19,893
So here's the question we started with.

276
00:16:20,393 --> 00:16:26,745
How could a magnitude 6.2 earthquake be
so much more deadly than the 7.1 that

277
00:16:26,745 --> 00:16:28,298
came before it?

278
00:16:28,798 --> 00:16:31,341
The answer comes down to four things.

279
00:16:31,841 --> 00:16:33,157
First, distance.

280
00:16:33,456 --> 00:16:36,280
The September quake struck
out among the farms.

281
00:16:36,360 --> 00:16:40,293
The February quake struck almost
directly beneath the city.

282
00:16:40,793 --> 00:16:42,105
Second, depth.

283
00:16:42,405 --> 00:16:47,432
It was shallow, so its energy reached the
surface with very little to soften it.

284
00:16:47,932 --> 00:16:49,226
Third, timing.

285
00:16:49,527 --> 00:16:53,437
At 4:35 in the morning,
the streets were empty.

286
00:16:53,518 --> 00:16:56,697
At 12:51 in the afternoon, they were full.

287
00:16:57,197 --> 00:16:59,948
And fourth, damage that was already there.

288
00:17:00,028 --> 00:17:04,815
Many buildings had been weakened by the
first earthquake and its thousands of

289
00:17:04,815 --> 00:17:05,863
aftershocks.

290
00:17:05,942 --> 00:17:09,843
Some had cracks that were patched
rather than fully understood.

291
00:17:09,923 --> 00:17:13,634
When the second blow came, they
had less strength left to give.

292
00:17:14,134 --> 00:17:15,678
A smaller earthquake.

293
00:17:15,978 --> 00:17:19,755
In exactly the wrong place,
at exactly the wrong time.

294
00:17:20,255 --> 00:17:22,584
That's what nearly ended Christchurch.

295
00:17:23,084 --> 00:17:27,497
And in the months that followed, it
truly felt like the city might not come

296
00:17:27,497 --> 00:17:28,096
back.

297
00:17:28,596 --> 00:17:31,982
The heart of Christchurch was
sealed off behind a cordon.

298
00:17:32,063 --> 00:17:34,914
Soldiers and police guarded the edges.

299
00:17:34,994 --> 00:17:39,778
The area became known as the red zone,
and parts of it stayed closed to the

300
00:17:39,778 --> 00:17:42,042
public for more than two years.

301
00:17:42,542 --> 00:17:46,818
Inside that cordon, the city
center was slowly taken apart.

302
00:17:46,898 --> 00:17:50,731
Building after building was
judged too dangerous to save.

303
00:17:50,811 --> 00:17:54,563
Most of the central city
was eventually demolished.

304
00:17:54,643 --> 00:17:58,886
Whole streets that people had walked
their entire lives simply vanished.

305
00:17:59,386 --> 00:18:04,451
Even the Hotel Grand Chancellor, the
leaning tower that had loomed over the

306
00:18:04,451 --> 00:18:07,167
rescue, was eventually brought down.

307
00:18:07,667 --> 00:18:10,586
In the east, the land
itself was written off.

308
00:18:10,666 --> 00:18:15,955
Around eight thousand homes along the
Avon River were placed in a residential

309
00:18:15,955 --> 00:18:16,884
red zone.

310
00:18:16,963 --> 00:18:22,644
The ground beneath them was considered
too damaged, too likely to liquefy again.

311
00:18:22,724 --> 00:18:26,705
Families who had lived there for
generations were forced to leave.

312
00:18:26,786 --> 00:18:32,097
Entire neighborhoods were cleared, house
by house, until nothing remained but

313
00:18:32,097 --> 00:18:34,866
empty streets and overgrown gardens.

314
00:18:35,366 --> 00:18:37,644
The financial cost was staggering.

315
00:18:37,724 --> 00:18:43,182
Official estimates put the total cost of
rebuilding at around forty billion New

316
00:18:43,182 --> 00:18:48,300
Zealand dollars, making it one of the
most expensive natural disasters the

317
00:18:48,300 --> 00:18:50,188
country has ever faced.

318
00:18:50,688 --> 00:18:52,976
And there were hard questions to answer.

319
00:18:53,476 --> 00:18:59,363
A Royal Commission investigated why so
many buildings had failed, and especially

320
00:18:59,363 --> 00:19:03,319
why the CTV building had
collapsed the way it did.

321
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:08,269
It found serious problems in how the
building had been designed and approved.

322
00:19:08,349 --> 00:19:13,726
Those findings led to major changes in
how New Zealand deals with buildings at

323
00:19:13,726 --> 00:19:15,403
risk in earthquakes.

324
00:19:15,903 --> 00:19:18,410
But a city is more than its buildings.

325
00:19:18,910 --> 00:19:24,128
Before the end of 2011, a temporary
shopping area made from brightly painted

326
00:19:24,128 --> 00:19:27,564
shipping containers
opened in the city center.

327
00:19:27,644 --> 00:19:32,589
It became a symbol that Christchurch
was still alive, still trading, still

328
00:19:32,589 --> 00:19:34,224
refusing to give up.

329
00:19:34,724 --> 00:19:37,409
Two years later, a new cathedral opened.

330
00:19:37,489 --> 00:19:43,481
Not made of stone this time, but built
using huge cardboard tubes, designed by

331
00:19:43,481 --> 00:19:46,159
Japanese architect Shigeru Ban.

332
00:19:46,239 --> 00:19:48,964
People called it the Cardboard Cathedral.

333
00:19:49,045 --> 00:19:54,064
Simple, light, and built to stand
while the city decided what came next.

334
00:19:54,564 --> 00:19:59,867
Along the Avon River, the abandoned red
zone neighborhoods slowly began turning

335
00:19:59,867 --> 00:20:00,582
green.

336
00:20:00,662 --> 00:20:05,934
The land that could no longer hold
houses started becoming parkland and open

337
00:20:05,934 --> 00:20:06,664
space.

338
00:20:07,164 --> 00:20:10,938
And beside that same river,
a memorial now stands.

339
00:20:11,018 --> 00:20:16,743
A long, curved wall carrying the
names of the 185 people who died.

340
00:20:16,824 --> 00:20:22,564
It was unveiled on the twenty-second
of February, 2017, six years to the day

341
00:20:22,564 --> 00:20:24,453
after the earthquake.

342
00:20:24,953 --> 00:20:27,425
Christchurch today is a different city.

343
00:20:27,506 --> 00:20:28,412
Newer.

344
00:20:28,711 --> 00:20:29,589
Lower.

345
00:20:29,890 --> 00:20:33,092
Built with the lessons of
that afternoon in mind.

346
00:20:33,592 --> 00:20:36,184
But beneath it, the ground hasn't changed.

347
00:20:36,684 --> 00:20:38,789
The sand and silt are still there.

348
00:20:38,869 --> 00:20:41,715
The Port Hills still
rise above the suburbs.

349
00:20:41,796 --> 00:20:46,842
And scientists know now that hidden
faults can sit quietly for thousands of

350
00:20:46,842 --> 00:20:50,211
years beneath places
that look perfectly safe.

351
00:20:50,711 --> 00:20:54,681
The ground beneath Christchurch
was silent for a very long time.

352
00:20:55,181 --> 00:20:57,933
Then, in five months, it woke up twice.

353
00:20:58,433 --> 00:21:03,202
And it only needed eighteen seconds
to remind everyone who was really in

354
00:21:03,202 --> 00:21:04,039
control.
